To install all the browser engines (Chromium, Firefox, and WebKit) inside your Python virtual environment on Ubuntu, you can use Playwright's built-in CLI command. Playwright manages its own isolated browser binaries so it won't mess up your personal system browsers.
Make sure your virtual environment is active (myvenv) and run the following command in your terminal:

playwright install

## 📦 What Happens Under the Hood?
This single command downloads and configures three distinct browser distributions directly into your local environment:

   1. Chromium (Used for standard Chrome/Edge emulation)
   2. Firefox (Mozilla's independent browser engine)
   3. WebKit (Apple's native Safari browser engine)

## 🐧 Crucial Step for Ubuntu Users: Installing System Dependencies
On Ubuntu, these headless browsers require specific low-level system libraries (like audio, video, and font packages) to launch without crashing. If you are running on a server or a fresh Ubuntu desktop, you should also run this command to automatically install all missing Linux dependencies via APT:

playwright install-deps

## 🧪 Quick Verification Test
Once the installation finishes, you can verify that all engines are active by writing a quick 3-line terminal Python execution command:

python3 -c "from playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright; p = sync_playwright().start(); print('Chromium:', p.chromium); print('Firefox:', p.firefox); print('WebKit:', p.webkit); p.stop()"

If it successfully prints the object pointers for all three browsers, your environment is ready to test scripts across different browser footprints!
